NNAAMMEE
       pamfind  -  Print  the locations of all tuples of a certain value in an
       image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammffiinndd { --ttaarrggeett==_s_a_m_p_l_e_0,,_s_a_m_p_l_e_1,, ... | --ccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r } [--mmaacchhiinnee] [_i_m_‐
       _a_g_e_f_i_l_e]

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one.  You may separate  an  option  name
       and its value with white space instead of an equals sign.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammffiinndd  reads a Netpbm image (PNM or PAM) and prints a list of all the
       locations (row and column) of the tuples that have a value you specify.
       For example, you can list all the places that a visual image is red.

       You  can specify the value in actual decimal sample values with a --ttaarr‐‐
       ggeett option or as a color with --ccoolloorr.  If you specify --ccoolloorr, the  pro‐
       gram fails if the input image does not have depth 3.  If it has depth 3
       but the tuples aren't actually colors, you get results as if they are.

       To do the opposite, see what tuple is at a given location,  use  ppaammccuutt
       and ppaammttaabbllee:


             $ pamcut -left=5 -top=7 -width=1 -height=1 | pamtable



       ppppmmccoolloorrmmaasskk  also finds all the tuples of a certain value, at least in
       visual images, but instead of printing their coordinates, it  generates
       a  mask image, which you can use to visualize where those tuples are or
       as input to another program.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppaammffiinndd recognizes the
       following command line options:




       --ttaarrggeett==_s_a_m_p_l_e_0,,_s_a_m_p_l_e_1,, ... |
              This specifies the tuple value to find.  You specify the  sample
              values  in  order,  and must specify the proper number of sample
              values for the depth of the image (e.g. 3 if it is a  color  im‐
              age).

              You must specify exactly one of --ttaarrggeett and --ccoolloorr.


       --ccoolloorr==_c_o_l_o_r
              This  is the color to find, assuming the image is a color visual
              image.

              _c_o_l_o_r is as described for the argument of  the  ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())
              library routine ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              You must specify exactly one of --ttaarrggeett and --ccoolloorr.


       --mmaacchhiinnee
              This  makes  the  output  more  convenient for a machine to use,
              while
                less convenient for a human.  ppaammffiinndd reports the same  infor‐
              mation
                as with no options.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.87 (March 2020).




